JustDogs - Top-5 products : weekly forecasting with 1st-order differencing where needed
  DATA / PRODUCTS / ENCODING : Arth.zip (sales_daily_train.csv, event calendar, run_model.py)
  TIME-SERIES FLOW           : project_contri.py

Per product (in this order):
  1. cleaning (Arth encoding) -> daily / weekly / monthly net sales, plots, day-of-week profile
  2. weekly decomposition, ADF stationarity test
       ADF p > 0.05  ->  weekly series is NON-stationary  ->  models are fitted on the 1st DIFFERENCE
                          (w_t = y_t - y_(t-1)) and the forecasts are added back up to sales level
       ADF p <= 0.05 ->  models are fitted on the weekly sales level
  3. Train/Test split, all methods evaluated (metrics always on the SALES level, so comparable)
  4. ONE GRAPH for each of the TOP-3 methods (Naive drawn on every graph)
  5. metrics table: top-3 methods (Naive is a normal, ranked method; shown with its rank if not in the top 3)
  6. best method (refit on all weeks before 1-Jun-2025) -> TRUE FORECAST June, July, Aug, Sep 2025
     (June actuals exist in the data; they are NOT used for training, only shown to check the June forecast)
     Stock-out rule: if a product ends with >= 4 empty weeks (no sales) those weeks are NOT learnt as "zero demand".
     The models are trained on the active period only, and the forecast then covers the gap weeks + Jul-Sep as
     DEMAND (what it would sell if available), so a forecast of 0 is never produced just because stock ran out.

Naive = previous week's actual value is this week's prediction (week after week).
Nothing is saved to disk: results are only printed and plots are only displayed.

Usage:
    python top5_forecast_top3_models.py [sales_daily_train.csv] [JustDogs_Master_Event_Calendar_2022_2025.csv]
Needs: pandas numpy matplotlib seaborn scikit-learn statsmodels   (lightgbm optional)
